{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-2_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, your stomach problems can definitely be related to both stress and eating habits.\n\nStress and anxiety are closely linked to digestive health. Emotional stress can alter digestive function, affecting stomach acid production, gut motility, and nutrient absorption. Chronic stress can lead to issues like nervous stomach, stomach pain, upset stomach, acid reflux, heartburn, and can worsen conditions like ulcers and Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS). The brain and gut are directly connected, meaning stress can easily impact gut function and even change gut microbiota.\n\nYour eating habits also play a significant role. Eating large meals, eating late at night, skipping meals, or having long gaps between meals can lead to acid buildup, heartburn, and indigestion. Certain foods like spicy, oily, high-fat, or processed foods, as well as carbonated drinks, caffeine, and alcohol, can aggravate stomach issues and contribute to acidity, gas, and bloating. Eating too quickly or not chewing food thoroughly can also cause problems.\n\nMaking lifestyle changes to manage stress and adopt healthier eating habits can significantly help alleviate stomach problems.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-06-08282:9": 0.5933772921562195, "clueweb22-en0031-21-00176:4": 0.5426198244094849, "clueweb22-en0034-74-02623:7": 0.5226815342903137, "clueweb22-en0007-06-08282:3": 0.5203360915184021, "clueweb22-en0020-53-11346:9": 0.5185408592224121, "clueweb22-en0028-55-01421:3": 0.5082729458808899, "clueweb22-en0021-78-10901:1": 0.49525487422943115, "clueweb22-en0020-71-04476:4": 0.47786006331443787, "clueweb22-en0018-50-07111:1": 0.4776460826396942, "clueweb22-en0020-17-15528:16": 0.47695210576057434, "clueweb22-en0007-16-15172:15": 0.46445050835609436, "clueweb22-en0038-68-03750:6": 0.46387264132499695, "clueweb22-en0007-29-11192:1": 0.458977252244