{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To improve the taste of your coffee while keeping it low in acid and sugar-free, consider the following methods:\n\n**For Lower Acidity:**\n*   **Choose your roast and brew method:** Opt for darker roasts, which are generally recommended for lower acidity and can be gentler on the stomach (Passages 1, 3, 5, 11, 16, 17, 20). Cold brew coffee is significantly less acidic than hot-brewed coffee, making it a good choice if you experience mild acid reflux (Passages 1, 3, 5, 6, 16, 17, 19, 20).\n*   **Additives to neutralize acidity and enhance flavor:**\n    *   A pinch of **baking soda** can neutralize acidity and smooth the flavor (Passages 1, 2, 3, 5, 9, 12, 16, 20).\n    *   **Almond milk** is alkaline and can help tone down coffee's natural acidity (Passages 1, 2, 3, 4, 16, 20).\n    *   A bit of **salt** can neutralize bitterness, giving your coffee a sweeter feel (Passages 3, 8, 15, 16, 20).\n    *   A teaspoon of **coconut oil** can reduce acidity while adding a creamy touch and rich flavor (Passage 5).\n*   **Consider bean type and processing:** Arabica beans tend to have a smoother, sweeter flavor (Passage 7). Decaf coffee processed with the mountain water method can also be lower in acid (Passages 3, 16, 19, 20). You can also look for coffee brands specifically marketed as low-acid (Passages 9, 10, 11, 14, 19).\n\n**For Sugar-Free Options:**\n*   **Avoid added sugars:** The healthiest way to enjoy coffee is black, as added sugars and flavored sweeteners contribute calories and can trigger more stomach acids (Passages 4, 6, 7, 17).\n*   **Natural Flavoring:** If you prefer not to drink it black, consider adding natural flavorings like a sprinkle of cocoa, which aligns with your preference for dark chocolate (Passage 4).", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0030-01-18753:9": 0.8821342587471008, "clueweb22-en0014-61-177