{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To prevent your coffee from tasting sour, which is often a sign of under-extraction, focus on a few key variables.\n\nFirstly, **grind size** is crucial. If your coffee tastes sour, your grind might be too coarse for your brewing method. A coarse grind allows water to pass through too quickly, leading to under-extraction of sugars and other balancing flavors, leaving behind the initial sour acids. Try making your grind finer, gradually adjusting until you achieve a balanced taste.\n\nSecondly, **brew time** plays a significant role. Under-extraction occurs when the coffee doesn't brew long enough. Increasing the brew time can help extract more of the desirable compounds, leading to a sweeter, less sour cup. However, be careful not to over-extract, which can cause bitterness.\n\nAdditionally, consider your **coffee-to-water ratio**. Using too much water or too little coffee can result in a weak, sour brew. Aim for a balanced ratio, ensuring enough coffee grounds for proper extraction.\n\nFinally, **water temperature** can impact the taste. If your coffee is consistently sour, try using hotter water, as insufficient temperature can also lead to under-extraction. If you've already brewed a sour cup, a pinch of salt can help counteract the sourness.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:15": 0.968755304813385, "clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:18": 0.9594559669494629, "clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:14": 0.9531363248825073, "clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:19": 0.9499800801277161, "clueweb22-en0002-51-06448:22": 0.9429993033409119, "clueweb22-en0004-93-04535:13": 0.9389153718948364, "clueweb22-en0000-23-14012:2": 0.9201293587684631, "clueweb22-en0004-93-04535:11": 0.9072285294532776, "clueweb22-en0002-51-06448:23": 0.8995099067687988, "clueweb22-en0035-48-11701:3": 0.8892638087272644, "clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:20": 0.8846720457077