{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Experiencing finger pain from climbing can be frustrating. To help manage it, initially, try resting the hand and applying ice for about 20 minutes a few times a day, ensuring not to apply ice directly to bare skin. You can also submerge your fingers in ice water for several minutes to aid healing. Massaging your fingers and forearms and maintaining a good diet can also reduce inflammation.\n\nFor prevention, warming up thoroughly is key, starting with aerobic activity, then dynamic stretching, and finally easy climbing. Focus on proper footwork and avoid intense dynamic movements, especially crimping, which increases injury risk. Post-climbing, engage in static stretching and use a balm or lotion to moisturize your hands.\n\nIf the pain persists or worsens after 48 to 72 hours of home care, or if you experience severe pain, swelling, redness, warmth, fever, or inability to move your finger, it's important to seek prompt medical attention. A climbing-knowledgeable physician can diagnose the injury, especially for pulley sprains, and guide your recovery, which might involve gentle range-of-motion exercises and gradual re-strengthening, avoiding crimping initially.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0023-44-07422:2": 0.5401575565338135, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 0.4997473359107971, "clueweb22-en0041-52-01391:3": 0.4277743995189667, "clueweb22-en0023-44-07422:3": 0.4122857451438904, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:11": 0.3373018503189087, "clueweb22-en0008-49-08589:1": 0.3281170427799225, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:6": 0.31141042709350586, "clueweb22-en0008-49-08589:4": 0.30274009704589844, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:5": 0.3000812530517578, "clueweb22-en0008-49-08589:2": 0.2917955815792084, "clueweb22-en0005-72-05634:1": 0.2790616750717163, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:4": 0.2788284718990326}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "refere