{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Given your finger pain might be related to climbing, it's important to know that finger injuries are common in the sport. These can include flexor tendon injuries, often affecting the pulleys (like A2 or A4) due to repeated use of small holds or crimp-grip positions. This is sometimes known as \"Climber's finger.\" You might experience pain when attempting to bend your finger, tenderness along the palm side, or even an inability to bend it.\n\nOther possibilities include ligament or capsular strains, where pain might be on the side of the joint, or muscle strains, especially after intense sessions. These injuries are often a result of overuse.\n\nIt's crucial to listen to your body. If you have acute soreness, pain, or if your finger feels \"tweaked,\" it's best to take time off from climbing immediately. Continuing to climb on an injured finger can worsen the condition and lead to longer recovery times.\n\nFor severe pain, significant swelling, pain that developed after an injury, or if you can't bend your finger, it's highly recommended to consult a doctor. Flexor tendon injuries, for example, often require repair within 7 to 10 days of the injury.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 0.7545408606529236, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:17": 0.6904726028442383, "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:12": 0.6062361598014832, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 0.5817428827285767, "clueweb22-en0041-52-01391:1": 0.5640785098075867, "clueweb22-en0043-12-04302:3": 0.5486388206481934, "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:5": 0.5419374704360962}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0027-85-12523:9": 0.7546231150627136, "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 0.7545408606529236, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:17": 0.6904726028442383, "clueweb22-en0027-85-01794:16": 0.671434223651886, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:3": 0.6634401679039001, "clue