{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Your fingers are intricate, and understanding the role of tendons can help pinpoint your pain. Tendons are fibrous connective tissues that attach muscles to bones, allowing your fingers to move. Interestingly, the muscles that control your finger movements are actually located in your palm and forearm; these muscles pull on the tendons in your fingers, which then make them move.\n\nThere are two main types of tendons in your hand: flexor tendons, on the palm side, which help bend your fingers, and extensor tendons, on the back of your hand, which help straighten them.\n\nIf your pain is related to a tendon, you might experience tenderness, especially over the palm side of your finger. Common symptoms of tendon injury or pain (often called tendinosis) include pain and tenderness, decreased strength, and difficulty with movement. For example, a torn flexor tendon can make it impossible to bend your finger, or if an extensor tendon is injured, your fingertip might noticeably droop and only straighten if you push it. Conditions like 'trigger finger,' which involves inflammation of the tendon sheath, can also make it difficult to bend or straighten the finger.\n\nTo help me understand better, could you describe:\n*   Exactly where on your finger the pain is located (e.g., palm side, back, near a specific joint)?\n*   What movements (bending, straightening, gripping) make the pain worse?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0028-83-11465:5": 0.41112613677978516, "clueweb22-en0041-10-08613:8": 0.5862728357315063, "clueweb22-en0037-41-07070:12": 0.4809451699256897, "clueweb22-en0030-77-07879:6": 0.4228724241256714, "clueweb22-en0045-22-05232:14": 0.5571890473365784, "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:7": 0.4089842140674591, "clueweb22-en0029-41-13424:2": 0.46053174138069153, "clueweb22-en0036-15-08986:3": 0.43780717253685}, "ptkb_provenan