{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, it is still possible that your pain is related to an A2 pulley injury, even if you don't see any bowstringing.\n\nA2 pulley strains are very common in climbers, especially when using a full crimp grip, which exerts significant force on the pulleys. The pain from these injuries is typically felt on the front of the finger, or at its base, and is often noticeable when trying to straighten or bend the finger, or when crimping.\n\nWhile bowstringing\u2014a bulging at the base of the finger\u2014can occur with more severe A2-A4 ruptures, its absence doesn't rule out a pulley injury. Not all pulley injuries result in visible bowstringing, particularly if they are not a complete rupture or a severe tear. Some individuals with pulley injuries may report barely seeing any bowstringing.\n\nGiven that your pain is on the front of the finger and is exacerbated by crimping, an A2 pulley injury remains a strong possibility. It's advisable to consult with a medical professional for an accurate diagnosis and appropriate treatment plan.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 0.7550577521324158, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": 0.5166044235229492, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 0.34778448939323425, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 0.28622347116470337, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:18": 0.21749569475650787, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:17": 0.1759595274925232, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:9": 0.16371116042137146}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 0.7550577521324158, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": 0.5166044235229492, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 0.34778448939323425, "clueweb22-en0000-18-09663:2": 0.30813780426979065, "clueweb22-en0000-18-09663:3": 0.29602986574172974, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 0.28622347116470337, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 0.2430848926305771, "clueweb22-en0012-60-1