{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Given your pain in the front of the finger when crimping, but without visible bowstringing, it sounds like you might have a pulley strain, possibly a Grade 1 or a partial tear. The first step is always to seek a diagnosis from a climbing-knowledgeable physician or orthopedic surgeon, as they can assess the damage and rule out other issues.\n\nIn the meantime, you can follow the R.I.C.E. protocol for muscle strains:\n*   **Rest**: Allow the finger to repair by minimizing use, especially avoiding activities that cause pain like crimping.\n*   **Ice**: Apply ice to the area for 10-15 minutes every hour for the first 24 hours, then every 3-4 hours to reduce swelling and pain.\n*   **Compression**: Use a compression wrap or tape to help prevent swelling and provide support.\n*   **Elevation**: Elevate your hand above your heart to reduce swelling.\n\nFor a Grade 1 pulley sprain, gentle range-of-motion exercises are recommended: bend and straighten your finger until it feels tight, hold for 2-3 seconds, relax, and repeat several times a day. Avoid pushing through pain. If you plan to continue climbing, H-taping can provide extra support to the injured pulley, helping to keep the tendons close to the bone. Over-the-counter anti-inflammatory drugs like ibuprofen can also help manage pain and inflammation if cleared by your doctor.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0024-41-05040:2": 0.3129005432128906, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:9": 0.27158433198928833, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": 0.2674039602279663, "clueweb22-en0000-20-10719:8": 0.2605139911174774, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 0.2542881965637207}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 0.8355603218078613, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": 0.4849340319633484, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 0.4785994589328766, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:15": 0.