{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Since you prefer not to take supplements, taping can be a helpful way to support your finger as you ease back into climbing. Research suggests that H-taping is the most effective method for supporting injured finger pulleys, helping to keep tendons close to the bone, especially when crimping. It can even increase crimping strength by an average of 13%.\n\nHere's how to apply H-taping:\n1. Cut a piece of rigid athletic tape (like Leukoplast brand) about 10cm long and 1.5cm wide.\n2. Cut the tape in half lengthwise from both ends, leaving a 1cm bridge in the middle.\n3. Place the tape on the inside (palm side) of your injured finger, with the bridge sitting on the crease of the joint above the injured pulley.\n4. Flex your finger about 60 degrees at the taped joint.\n5. Wrap the upper part of the tape around your finger to form a ring, then wrap the lower part to form another ring below the joint.\n\nRemember to retape your finger between each climb, as the tape stretches and its support can diminish quickly. While H-taping provides support, another approach, advocated by Dr. Julian Saunders, focuses on complete immobilization. For this, you tape your finger straight from halfway between the first two creases to halfway between the second and third, overlapping 50%, to prevent any crimping.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": 0.9581419825553894, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 0.9294650554656982, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": 0.8805570006370544, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 0.8745455145835876, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:10": 0.8224632740020752, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:11": 0.7866395711898804, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:13": 0.7120155096054077, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": 0.6933505535125732, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 0.6601141095161438, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 0.6437700390815735, "cluew