{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "H-taping is a highly effective method for supporting injured finger pulleys in climbers, especially for preventing reinjury and helping tendons stay close to the bone when crimping. Research shows it is more effective than other methods like X or circular taping, and can even increase the crimping strength of injured fingers by an average of 13%.\n\nTo apply H-taping:\n1.  Cut a length of rigid athletic tape (e.g., Leukoplast brand) approximately 10cm long and 1.5cm wide (or a 4-inch piece from the full width of the roll).\n2.  Cut the tape in half lengthwise from both ends, leaving a 1cm (or \u00bd\u201d) bridge in the middle.\n3.  Place the tape on the injured finger with the bridge on the inside (palmar side), sitting on the crease of the joint on the upper end of the injured pulley.\n4.  Flex your finger 60\u00b0 (or 30\u00b0) at the joint being taped.\n5.  Wrap the upper part of the tape in a ring, then wrap the other ring of tape below the joint.\n\nIt's important to retape your finger between climbs or whenever the tape feels loose, as tape stretches quickly and its support can diminish. H-taping is generally recommended over methods like buddy taping for pulley injuries.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": 0.861500084400177, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 0.8386898040771484, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:10": 0.6692042946815491, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 0.5500901341438293, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:9": 0.503662645816803, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": 0.46119269728660583, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:14": 0.43869972229003906, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 0.4213363528251648, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:10": 0.3141699731349945}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": 0.861500084400177, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 0.8386898040771484, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796: