{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Choosing the right running shoes is crucial for comfort, performance, and injury prevention. The most important factor is **comfort** \u2013 the shoes should feel good from the moment you try them on. Don't be swayed by brand names or sales pitches; try on several pairs and jog around the store to see how they feel (12, 15).\n\nEnsure the **fit** is not too snug, allowing a thumb's width between your longest toe and the shoe's end, as your foot will splay upon impact. Running shoes are often a half to full size larger than your casual shoes. It's best to try them on with the socks you intend to run in (3, 5, 10). If possible, get your shoes professionally fitted at a running specialty store (5, 20).\n\nLook for shoes that offer sufficient **support** and **cushioning** to absorb shock and protect your joints. The type of support you need (neutral, stability, or motion control) depends on your foot type and pronation (how your foot rolls inward). Shoes should also be **lightweight** and **flexible** (1, 4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 14, 17, 19). Durability is also a key factor, especially for long mileage (8, 17).\n\nFinally, remember to break in your new shoes. Never use a brand new pair for a race; they should have at least 100 miles on them beforehand (1, 8).", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0026-47-01829:12": 0.9813516736030579, "clueweb22-en0032-57-03681:10": 0.9804701209068298, "clueweb22-en0029-41-08595:8": 0.9678630828857422, "clueweb22-en0032-57-03681:11": 0.9661450982093811, "clueweb22-en0026-47-01829:13": 0.965238094329834, "clueweb22-en0040-62-14515:1": 0.9596148133277893, "clueweb22-en0026-47-01829:11": 0.9595025181770325, "clueweb22-en0004-12-10209:10": 0.9589875936508179, "clueweb22-en0029-92-05545:9": 0.9499135613441467, "clueweb22-en0007-06-09660:6": 0.9463903307914734, "clueweb22-en0022-19-11828:8": 0.940303385257721