{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, your diet will need to change during pregnancy to support both your health and your baby's development. It's recommended to eat a healthy, balanced, and varied diet focusing on nutrient-dense, whole foods. This includes plenty of fruits and vegetables, whole grains, lean proteins, and dairy products.\n\nWhile you don't need to \"eat for two,\" your calorie needs will increase in the second and third trimesters. It's crucial to get enough key nutrients like calcium (aim for 1,000-1,300 mg daily), iron (27 mg daily), iodine (220 mcg daily), and folic acid (400 mcg daily). Taking a prenatal vitamin supplement is highly recommended, but discuss it with your doctor.\n\nRegarding your interest in low-carb foods, it's important to avoid carb-restrictive diets like keto or Atkins during pregnancy, as your baby needs carbohydrates for development. Instead, choose complex carbohydrates like whole-grain breads, brown rice, and beans over simple ones like white bread.\n\nYou should also avoid certain foods due to potential risks, including raw or undercooked meats and eggs, unpasteurized dairy products and juices, raw seafood, and high-mercury fish like shark, swordfish, and marlin. Limit sugary foods, processed items, and excessive caffeine. Staying well-hydrated by drinking plenty of water (around 8-10 cups daily) is also very important.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0008-41-08982:10": 0.9768721461296082, "clueweb22-en0040-08-14240:10": 0.9739884734153748, "clueweb22-en0018-28-09879:1": 0.9701356887817383, "clueweb22-en0027-79-08948:2": 0.9700286388397217, "clueweb22-en0008-96-17009:5": 0.9675169587135315, "clueweb22-en0008-96-17009:6": 0.9646791815757751, "clueweb22-en0001-10-19534:7": 0.963361918926239, "clueweb22-en0026-42-08361:1": 0.9624053239822388, "clueweb22-en0023-20-17679:9": 0.9605302810668945, "clueweb22-en00