{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "During pregnancy, focusing on a healthy, balanced diet is crucial for both you and your baby. It's a common misconception that you need to \"eat for two\"; instead, calorie needs increase gradually. You generally don't need extra calories in the first trimester, but around 300-350 additional calories per day in the second, and about 500 more in the third.\n\nAim for a variety of nutrient-dense foods, including fruits, vegetables, whole grains, lean proteins (like meat, fish, poultry, eggs, beans, nuts), and dairy products. These provide essential nutrients such as protein, fiber, calcium (1000-1300 mg/day), iron (27 mg/day), and iodine (220 mcg/day).\n\nIt's important to avoid certain foods to prevent foodborne illnesses or harm to your baby. Steer clear of raw or undercooked meats, eggs, and seafood; unpasteurized dairy products and juices; soft cheeses; and high-mercury fish like shark, swordfish, or marlin. Limit liver due to high Vitamin A content. Reduce sugary foods, simple carbohydrates, and excessive caffeine.\n\nStay well-hydrated by drinking about 8-10 glasses of water daily. It is strongly recommended to take a daily prenatal vitamin, which typically includes folic acid (400 mcg, especially until week 12) and Vitamin D. Always discuss your dietary needs and any supplements with your healthcare provider.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0023-54-04375:6": 0.980518102645874, "clueweb22-en0008-41-08982:10": 0.9768721461296082, "clueweb22-en0040-08-14240:10": 0.9739884734153748, "clueweb22-en0018-28-09879:1": 0.9701356887817383, "clueweb22-en0027-79-08948:2": 0.9700286388397217, "clueweb22-en0008-96-17009:5": 0.9675169587135315, "clueweb22-en0001-10-19534:8": 0.9658088088035583, "clueweb22-en0008-96-17009:6": 0.9646791815757751, "clueweb22-en0001-10-19534:7": 0.963361918926239, "clueweb22-en0026-42-08361:1": 0.96