{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "While breastfeeding, it's important to maintain a balanced and nutrient-dense diet to support both your health and your baby's. Your nutritional needs are increased during this period, similar to or even exceeding pregnancy.\n\nFor **protein**, it's recommended that breastfeeding mothers consume around 65-71 grams per day, or about 25 grams more than usual. Good sources include lean meats, poultry, fish, eggs, beans, lentils, nuts, seeds, and tofu. Protein helps build and repair body tissues and is crucial for milk supply.\n\nRegarding **calcium**, your needs are elevated, typically ranging from 1,000 to 1,300 mg daily. This is vital because your body can pull calcium from your bones to ensure your baby gets enough through breast milk. Excellent dietary sources include low-fat dairy products like milk, yogurt, and cheese, as well as leafy greens, legumes, almonds, and calcium-fortified fruit juices or plant-based milks.\n\nAs for **low-carb** diets, while you need carbohydrates for energy, completely eliminating them is not recommended. A moderate-carb, high-fiber diet can help with weight management without compromising your energy levels. Focus on whole grains, fruits, and vegetables for your carbohydrate intake.\n\nOverall, aim for a wide variety of foods from all food groups, including colorful fruits and vegetables, whole grains, healthy fats (like those in olive oil, avocados, and fatty fish), and lean proteins. You'll also notice increased hunger and thirst, so ensure you stay well-hydrated by drinking plenty of water throughout the day. Your calorie needs will be higher while breastfeeding, so listen to your body and eat enough to satisfy your appetite with nutrient-rich foods.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0009-78-08482:1": 0.9784392714500427, "clueweb22-en0034-54-11087:7": 0.971947968006134, "clueweb22-en0