{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, drinking alcohol while breastfeeding can impact your milk supply. Studies show that alcohol can reduce the amount of breast milk you produce and affect the let-down reflex, which is responsible for milk release. This happens because alcohol can disrupt the balance of hormones, like prolactin and oxytocin, that control milk production.\n\nEven a single drink can temporarily reduce your milk supply, and babies may consume less milk after their mother has had alcohol. For instance, some studies indicate a 20-23% decrease in milk intake by infants. Contrary to popular myths, drinking beer does not increase milk supply.\n\nWhile the immediate effects are temporary, chronic or heavy alcohol use has the potential to lower your overall milk supply and may lead to a shorter duration of breastfeeding. If your milk supply is reduced, your baby might seem hungrier and need to feed more often.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0031-57-16983:0": 0.9556519985198975, "clueweb22-en0002-39-11926:5": 0.9393941164016724, "clueweb22-en0019-08-09434:1": 0.9253653287887573, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:13": 0.9112891554832458, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:8": 0.8965374827384949, "clueweb22-en0005-68-03159:3": 0.8771131038665771, "clueweb22-en0005-68-03159:4": 0.8769136071205139, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:9": 0.873637318611145, "clueweb22-en0004-53-11196:3": 0.8722352385520935}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0031-57-16983:0": 0.9556519985198975, "clueweb22-en0002-39-11926:5": 0.9393941164016724, "clueweb22-en0042-99-06708:12": 0.9322140216827393, "clueweb22-en0019-08-09434:1": 0.9253653287887573, "clueweb22-en0019-08-09434:2": 0.922503650188446, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:13": 0.9112891554832458, "clueweb22-en0024-10-07042:1": 0.9047548174858093, "clueweb22-en0044-60-12234:19": 0.89942866563797, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11