{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "It's completely normal for babies to cry, and it's their main way of communicating their needs early on. Most newborns cry for about 2 to 3 hours a day, especially in their first few months. This crying typically peaks around 6 to 8 weeks of age. While it can be distressing, it's important to remember that this is a normal developmental stage.\n\nThe good news is that the amount of crying usually starts to decrease by around 3 months of age, and by 4 to 6 months at the latest, most babies have outgrown excessive crying. Sometimes, babies cry for no obvious reason, especially in the late afternoon and early evening, a period often called the \"witching hour.\"\n\nSome babies may cry much more, which could be a sign of colic. Colic is defined as crying for more than 3 hours a day, more than 3 days a week, for at least 3 weeks, in an otherwise healthy baby. Colic usually begins around 2 to 4 weeks and often resolves by 3 to 4 months. If you're concerned about your baby's crying or finding it hard to cope, it's always a good idea to speak with your health visitor or GP.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0038-57-04444:0": 0.9862383604049683, "clueweb22-en0026-29-08825:3": 0.984266459941864, "clueweb22-en0026-56-09659:2": 0.9744315147399902, "clueweb22-en0000-85-09747:5": 0.9677107334136963, "clueweb22-en0001-28-08060:7": 0.9617506861686707, "clueweb22-en0000-45-00661:0": 0.9599542021751404, "clueweb22-en0029-92-05890:2": 0.9589821100234985, "clueweb22-en0026-56-09659:1": 0.9580543637275696, "clueweb22-en0045-30-05786:1": 0.9551455974578857, "clueweb22-en0001-87-12954:4": 0.9481776356697083, "clueweb22-en0029-12-11547:4": 0.9442408084869385, "clueweb22-en0000-45-00661:11": 0.9420109987258911, "clueweb22-en0017-47-11265:4": 0.9414197206497192, "clueweb22-en0016-87-16257:2": 0.9397799372673035, "clueweb22-en0042-11-04140:3": 0