{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "6-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "The main difference between fully ablative and fractional CO2 laser treatments lies in how they target the skin, their intensity, and recovery time.\n\n**Fully Ablative CO2 Lasers:**\nThese treatments completely remove the entire top layer of skin in the treated area, while also heating deeper layers to stimulate collagen and elastin production. They are more intense and aggressive, typically requiring general anesthesia. Downtime is significant, usually a minimum of two weeks, with the skin being swollen, scabbed, and raw during healing. One treatment often suffices for desired results, but they carry a higher risk of pigmentation issues and are generally recommended for fair or light skin tones.\n\n**Fractional CO2 Lasers:**\nIn contrast, fractional CO2 lasers deliver a partial beam of light, targeting only a fraction of the skin in a pixelated pattern. This creates microscopic columns of treated tissue while leaving surrounding skin intact, allowing for faster healing. They are considerably less aggressive, usually requiring only local anesthesia. Downtime is much shorter, often just a matter of days. While they offer surface-level improvement for concerns like fine lines, texture, and hyperpigmentation, most people need a series of treatments for optimal results. Fractional lasers are newer, popular due to minimized pain, downtime, and risks.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:10": 0.9917844533920288, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:9": 0.934853196144104, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:12": 0.9190477132797241, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:11": 0.8953569531440735, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:13": 0.6994383931159973, "clueweb22-en0031-16-03068:3": 0.6202965974807739, "clueweb22-en0039-50-11586:13": 0.6119187474250793, "clueweb22-en0039-60-07521:4": 0.5989645719528198, "clueweb22-en0025-73-01656:9": 0.5968437790870667