{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "6-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Microdermabrasion generally has minimal side effects if performed correctly. You might experience slight irritation, redness, mild inflammation, or a sensation similar to sunburn for a few days. Rare side effects can include infection, bruising, pinpoint bleeding, or scarring if the procedure is performed too aggressively. It's also important to note that microdermabrasion can spread existing skin infections or reactivate dormant viruses like herpes.\n\nFor chemical peels, the side effects vary depending on the peel's depth. Light to medium peels commonly cause redness, swelling, stinging, burning, and flaking. Your skin will also be sensitive to sunlight for several days or weeks, making broad-spectrum SPF essential. Deeper peels carry more severe risks, including intense burning, throbbing, significant swelling (even eyelids swelling shut), blistering, cysts, white spots, and a higher risk of infection and scarring. There's also a risk of temporary or permanent skin discoloration, especially for darker skin tones, and a demarcation line between treated and untreated areas. In very rare and extreme cases, deep chemical peels have been associated with more severe systemic complications like heart, kidney, or liver damage.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0033-06-10590:10": 0.8331257104873657, "clueweb22-en0033-06-10590:9": 0.7833353877067566, "clueweb22-en0036-14-13056:11": 0.771841824054718, "clueweb22-en0012-20-04821:7": 0.7315528392791748, "clueweb22-en0032-47-03490:7": 0.7225816249847412, "clueweb22-en0033-06-10590:7": 0.6468052268028259, "clueweb22-en0004-95-05610:11": 0.6405355930328369, "clueweb22-en0024-80-03963:1": 0.538635790348053, "clueweb22-en0033-06-10590:4": 0.5341910123825073, "clueweb22-en0023-28-09850:10": 0.5119616389274597, "clueweb22-en0027-79-08527:13": 0.5060705542564392, "clueweb22-en0032-47-034