{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "7-1_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Baking bread at home is a rewarding experience, and it's easier than you might think, especially since you already have some basic baking experience! Many find it therapeutic, and the aroma of fresh bread filling your home is truly delightful.\n\nOne of the biggest advantages of baking your own bread is having complete control over the ingredients. You can ensure it's made with fresh, wholesome components, free from the preservatives often found in store-bought loaves. This allows you to customize it to your taste, whether you want to add nuts, seeds, or even make it whole-grain or gluten-free.\n\nFor a simple start, a bread maker can be incredibly helpful. Most breads require just five main ingredients: flour, yeast, liquid, salt, and sweetener. With a bread maker, you simply add your ingredients (liquids first, then dry, then yeast), select the settings, and press start. It can have a hot loaf ready in as little as an hour, and many machines also have settings for dough, cakes, or jams.\n\nIf you prefer a more hands-on approach, you can mix the dough yourself and bake it in a regular oven. Key tips for success include using fresh ingredients, especially yeast, and measuring accurately. Remember that yeast dough often needs time to rise, sometimes twice, in a warm environment. Patience is key for a fluffy, tender loaf. Once baked, let your bread rest for at least an hour before slicing to prevent it from drying out.\n\nNo matter which method you choose, baking bread at home is a wonderful way to enjoy fresh, delicious, and nutritious bread, perfect for sharing with family and friends!", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0031-30-02300:7": 0.7049998044967651, "clueweb22-en0013-99-03717:7": 0.6365052461624146, "clueweb22-en0031-00-03222:8": 0.6093975901603699, "clueweb22-en0022-60-14475:8": 0.6019279956817627, "clueweb22-en