{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "7-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To make bread healthy when baking at home, you have full control over the ingredients. Focus on using 100% whole grain or sprouted-grain flours, such as whole wheat, spelt, or rye, as these are rich in fiber and nutrients. Avoid bleached or enriched flours, and skip added sugars, preservatives, and unhealthy vegetable oils.\n\nYou can significantly boost the nutritional value by incorporating healthy \"add-ins.\" Consider flax seeds, oats, other seeds like chia, poppy, or sunflower, nuts, and even dried fruits or nutritious herbs and spices. These additions increase fiber, healthy fats (like omega-3s from flax and chia), and overall nutrient content. Recipes often use natural sweeteners like honey or maple syrup.\n\nSpecific healthy bread types you can bake include:\n*   **Whole Wheat Bread:** A classic, high in fiber and protein.\n*   **Flax Bread:** Packed with fiber and omega-3 fatty acids.\n*   **Sprouted Grain Breads (like Ezekiel):** Made from grains that have begun to sprout, offering more nutrients and less gluten.\n*   **Spelt Bread:** An ancient grain with a nutty flavor and good fiber.\n*   **Sourdough:** The fermentation process can make it easier to digest and increase nutrient availability.\n\nBaking your own ensures freshness and freedom from unwanted additives, making it a healthier choice overall.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0032-41-11013:2": 0.9380797147750854, "clueweb22-en0001-71-08228:8": 0.936360776424408, "clueweb22-en0001-71-08228:7": 0.902789294719696, "clueweb22-en0014-97-06160:21": 0.809118390083313, "clueweb22-en0002-99-00262:8": 0.7991276979446411, "clueweb22-en0034-70-18519:11": 0.7533771991729736, "clueweb22-en0028-12-02611:7": 0.7277511358261108, "clueweb22-en0032-41-11013:1": 0.6946271657943726, "clueweb22-en0023-51-05417:11": 0.6540669798851013, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:8": 0.62