{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "7-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For managing fatty liver, focusing on high-fiber, whole-grain bread options with minimal processing is key. Here are some recommendations:\n\n*   **Rye Bread:** Often considered one of the best choices due to its high fiber content and fewer carbs than regular white bread. It can be bitter, so mixing rye flour with whole wheat flour is a common and tasty approach.\n*   **Whole Wheat Bread:** Opt for 100% whole wheat bread made with simple ingredients like flour, yeast, salt, and water. It's much better than white flour as it's unbleached and provides more fiber.\n*   **Keto Bread:** If you enjoy baking, making your own keto bread is a good option, as many store-bought versions may contain less liver-friendly preservatives.\n*   **Sprouted Bread:** Made from sprouted whole grains, this type offers more nutrients and can help stabilize blood sugar levels.\n*   **Other Whole Grain Options:** Look for breads labeled as whole-grain, multi-grain, stone ground, pita, or sourdough.\n\nWhen choosing any bread, ensure it's free from added sugars, artificial sweeteners, and preservatives. Baking your own bread gives you the most control over ingredients. Regardless of the type, moderation is important; aim for a maximum of 2-3 slices per day to support your liver's recovery. Avoid white bread and other refined carbohydrates, as they can negatively impact fatty liver.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:7": 0.987504780292511, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:11": 0.9835790991783142, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:2": 0.9503113031387329, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:10": 0.9313550591468811, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:6": 0.8991051912307739, "clueweb22-en0025-78-05449:1": 0.8798697590827942, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:5": 0.8672816157341003, "clueweb22-en0034-02-17207:12": 0.8653916120529175, "clueweb22-en0032-18-12680:3": 0.84674131