{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "7-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For managing fatty liver, choosing the right type of bread is important. You should avoid white bread due to its simple carbohydrates, which can contribute to insulin resistance and fat buildup in the liver.\n\nInstead, focus on healthier alternatives like:\n*   **Rye Bread:** This is a good option as it's high in fiber and lower in carbs. Pure rye bread can be bitter and dense, so a common approach is to mix rye flour with whole wheat flour (e.g., 150 grams of rye flour with 200 grams of whole wheat flour, plus dry yeast, salt, and water). When buying, ensure it only contains base ingredients and no added sugars or preservatives.\n*   **Whole Wheat Bread:** A classic choice, it's better than white flour because it's unbleached and has more fiber, which slows sugar absorption. Look for 100% whole wheat bread with simple ingredients: flour, yeast, salt, and water.\n*   **Keto Bread:** This can be a good option, but it's highly recommended to bake your own to avoid the preservatives and chemicals often found in store-bought versions.\n*   **Sprouted Bread:** Made from sprouted whole grains, it offers more nutrients, lower carb counts, and helps stabilize blood sugar levels.\n\nRegardless of the type, it's crucial to consume bread in moderation. Aim for a maximum of 2-3 slices per day to help your liver recover and heal.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:7": 0.986164927482605, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:11": 0.9752006530761719, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:2": 0.9295694828033447, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:10": 0.9241794943809509, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:5": 0.9044384956359863, "clueweb22-en0034-02-17207:12": 0.8969690799713135, "clueweb22-en0025-78-05449:1": 0.8790109157562256, "clueweb22-en0012-09-04746:11": 0.8704437017440796, "clueweb22-en0014-11-18570:3": 0.8252658843994141}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "r