{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "It's completely understandable to feel that way. Jumping between tasks without finishing them is a very common challenge for individuals with ADHD when faced with multiple responsibilities. The key is to shift from multitasking to single-task focus.\n\nA highly effective strategy is to commit to doing just one task at a time. Try not to start a new project until your current one is fully completed. This helps prevent the feeling of being scattered and ensures you see tasks through. It's also important to avoid overscheduling yourself with too many projects at once. Practicing saying \"no\" to new tasks when your plate is already full can significantly help you stay focused on what's at hand.\n\nConsider using techniques like the \"Only Handle It Once\" (O.H.I.O) principle, where you aim to complete a task as soon as you start dealing with it. Setting a timer for focused work blocks, such as 15-minute intervals, can also train your brain to concentrate on a single task for that duration, helping you build momentum and complete items before moving on.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:8": 0.9546639323234558, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:10": 0.9308351278305054, "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:3": 0.9275910258293152, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:11": 0.919102668762207, "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:5": 0.9124786853790283, "clueweb22-en0031-34-14667:27": 0.851205587387085, "clueweb22-en0001-03-06965:5": 0.8309531211853027, "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:7": 0.8200768828392029, "clueweb22-en0019-75-08588:1": 0.8176724314689636}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:8": 0.9546639323234558, "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:4": 0.9458165764808655, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:10": 0.9308351278305054, "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:3": 0.9275910258293152, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:9": 0.9195034503936768, "cluewe