{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Dealing with distractions like emails and meetings during hyperfocus is crucial for maintaining your flow. The key is to proactively manage these interruptions.\n\nFor emails, a highly effective strategy is to turn off all notifications and pop-ups. Instead of reacting to every new message, dedicate specific, pre-scheduled times in your day to check and respond to emails. You can even set a timer for these \"email sprints\" to process them efficiently. Consider using an autoresponder for urgent requests, directing people to call you if something truly can't wait. Some people find it helpful to log out of their email client or delete the app from their phone during deep work sessions to reduce the temptation to check.\n\nFor meetings, utilize electronic reminders to ensure you don't miss scheduled appointments. During the meeting, taking notes can help you stay engaged and prevent restlessness. If possible, schedule meetings during times when you're less likely to be in a hyperfocus state, perhaps later in the afternoon. You can also be candid with your teammates about your focused work blocks, letting them know when you're less available for immediate interruptions.\n\nGenerally, to protect your hyperfocus, use tools like \"do not disturb\" mode on your phone and computer, close unnecessary browser tabs, and use noise-canceling headphones if any ambient sounds become distracting. The goal is to create a \"bubble\" around your hyperfocus.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:8": 0.9233013987541199, "clueweb22-en0041-12-03522:5": 0.9103772044181824, "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:4": 0.8524991869926453, "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:5": 0.8410987854003906, "clueweb22-en0000-56-03635:8": 0.8235129117965698, "clueweb22-en0037-12-00061:9": 0.7788724303245544, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:7": 0.7780993580818176, "clueweb22-