{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Dealing with scheduled meetings when you're in hyperfocus can definitely be tricky, as you don't want to break that valuable flow unnecessarily. Here are a few strategies to manage meetings without completely derailing your focus:\n\nFirst, consider **scheduling your meetings strategically**. Some people find it effective to designate specific days or parts of days just for meetings, leaving other days as \"meeting-free\" for deep work and hyperfocus. For example, you could try to schedule all your meetings on Mondays and Fridays, keeping Tuesdays, Wednesdays, and Thursdays as interruption-free as possible. Another approach is to schedule meetings in the late afternoon, as this time might be less ideal for your peak hyperfocus periods.\n\nSecond, **prepare for the transition**. If you know a meeting is coming up while you're in hyperfocus, set a timer (e.g., 15 minutes) before the meeting is due to start. This gives you a gentle nudge to begin transitioning your focus. For a stronger alert, you could use a loud alarm that requires you to physically get up to turn it off, which helps break the hyperfocus and prompts you to shift gears.\n\nLastly, **build in buffer time**. Try to schedule meetings with a few minutes of buffer time before and after, or suggest shorter meeting durations (e.g., 25 minutes instead of 30). This allows you to mentally prepare for the meeting and then decompress or re-orient yourself afterward without feeling rushed or abruptly pulled from your work. You can also communicate with colleagues about your focused work times, letting them know when you'll be available for interruptions.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0009-24-15142:3": 0.4895397424697876, "clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:8": 0.45392751693725586, "clueweb22-en0029-24-18238:8": 0.4141697883605957, "clueweb22-en0006-00-08364:15": 0.32646885