{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "It's completely understandable that meetings can feel like a major distraction, especially when you're in a state of hyperfocus. The key is to manage these interruptions proactively.\n\nOne effective strategy is to compartmentalize your workweek. You could dedicate specific days or blocks of time solely for meetings, protecting your other days for uninterrupted deep work and hyperfocus. This way, you mentally prepare for when interruptions are scheduled.\n\nWhen a meeting is approaching and you're deep in hyperfocus, your brain needs time to switch gears. Try setting a timer for 15 minutes before the meeting is due to start. When it rings, use that time to gently transition out of your hyperfocus mode. You can also communicate with your colleagues about your focused work periods, letting them know when you'll be unavailable for interruptions and when you'll be available again. This sets clear expectations and helps reduce unexpected distractions.\n\nFinally, you can make meetings themselves less disruptive by always reviewing the agenda beforehand and questioning the necessity or attendance of recurring meetings to ensure they are truly valuable and don't unnecessarily break your flow.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:8": 0.48819172382354736, "clueweb22-en0041-12-03522:7": 0.3352504074573517, "clueweb22-en0022-33-08001:9": 0.2984670102596283, "clueweb22-en0006-62-03234:3": 0.2740113139152527, "clueweb22-en0029-24-18238:8": 0.22938445210456848}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0041-12-03522:5": 0.5483720898628235, "clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:8": 0.48819172382354736, "clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:11": 0.438422828912735, "clueweb22-en0033-68-02460:14": 0.3457915782928467, "clueweb22-en0041-12-03522:7": 0.3352504074573517, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:8": 0.31201764941215515, "clueweb22-en0034-