{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To manage transitions around meetings and reset your focus, here are some strategies you can try:\n\n**Before Meetings:**\n*   **Prepare Mentally:** Take a few minutes to get into a good headspace. This could involve making notes of things you don't want to forget, having a solitary cup of tea, or simply clearing your mind of other distractions.\n*   **Short Meditation/Breathing:** Use the minutes before a meeting for a brief meditation or deep breathing exercise to help you find your focus and calm your mind.\n\n**After Meetings:**\n*   **Micro-Breaks/Buffer Zones:** Instead of jumping straight into the next task, create a \"buffer zone.\" Step away from your desk, shake out your body, take a few deep breaths, stretch, or even step outside for a moment. This physical change of location and brief mental reset can be very effective.\n*   **Reboot with Movement/Meditation:** Use these short breaks to move your body (e.g., a few jumping jacks or stretches) or engage in a short meditation to reboot your energy and help your brain transition back to focused work.\n*   **Schedule Buffers:** If possible, suggest or implement shorter meeting durations (e.g., 45-50 minutes instead of an hour) to naturally build in these crucial transition times between calls.\n\nThese small, intentional breaks can significantly help in resetting your mental energy and focus, especially when dealing with ADHD.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0023-69-08765:9": 0.8830143213272095, "clueweb22-en0009-21-04763:5": 0.7640562057495117, "clueweb22-en0009-21-04763:6": 0.6968773603439331, "clueweb22-en0022-70-00626:5": 0.5830650925636292, "clueweb22-en0021-80-10230:3": 0.5629675388336182, "clueweb22-en0041-38-03890:9": 0.5390997529029846, "clueweb22-en0017-46-05051:7": 0.5244156122207642, "clueweb22-en0041-38-03890:8": 0.5181078910827637, "clueweb22-en0