{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-2_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To get started with tattooing, the most common and highly valued route is to secure an apprenticeship at a tattoo shop. Under the guidance of a professional tattoo artist, you will learn drawing techniques, develop your personal style, and master the use and sterilization of essential equipment like needles and ink.\n\nBefore pursuing an apprenticeship, it's crucial to cultivate strong artistic skills. While formal training isn't strictly necessary, associate or bachelor's degrees in visual and fine arts can significantly refine your abilities and expose you to various artistic styles, which is beneficial for designing diverse tattoos. Courses in color theory, figure drawing, and 2-dimensional drawing are particularly helpful.\n\nYou'll need to build an impressive portfolio showcasing your artwork and design ideas, ideally tailored towards tattoo designs, as apprenticeships are competitive. Practice is key; refine your drawing skills on paper, and then move on to practicing on fake skin or even fruit like apples and pears to get a feel for the medium. Additionally, developing a keen eye for detail, strong hand-eye coordination, and a commitment to hygiene are essential for a successful career in tattooing. Licensing is also required in most states.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0025-87-09139:2": 0.3981432020664215, "clueweb22-en0025-87-09139:1": 0.32544922828674316, "clueweb22-en0007-52-05511:16": 0.28630316257476807, "clueweb22-en0015-05-03360:4": 0.27006885409355164, "clueweb22-en0024-22-17473:2": 0.2130250185728073, "clueweb22-en0025-87-09139:3": 0.20167137682437897, "clueweb22-en0025-87-09139:0": 0.19077065587043762, "clueweb22-en0024-21-00136:8": 0.18631893396377563, "clueweb22-en0024-21-00136:1": 0.18076030910015106, "clueweb22-en0024-21-00136:7": 0.17504195868968964, "clueweb22-en0028-52-08765:6": 0.165384322