{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For improving hand comfort on your mountain bike, you have several options for both handlebars and grips.\n\nWhen it comes to **grips**, look for ergonomic designs that reduce pressure on your hands and wrists, especially helpful for preventing numbness or pain on longer rides or rough terrain. Many feature special grooves, textures, or \"mini-wing\" shapes to support your hand and wrist in an ideal position, absorb shock, and provide a strong, anti-slip hold even in wet or muddy conditions. Some grips also offer wrist correction and extra palm cushioning. Lock-on grips are recommended as they prevent slipping on the bars.\n\nFor **handlebars**, consider riser bars. These slope upward at the sides, lifting your hands to a higher, more upright position. This can take some weight off your hands, improve control on steep terrain, and put your wrists in a more comfortable position, which is beneficial for riding on hills and rough paths. While flat bars keep you in a more crouched position, riser bars are generally preferred for mountain biking comfort. The \"sweep\" or bend of the handlebars also plays a role in wrist comfort. Additionally, bar ends are optional extensions that can provide multiple hand positions, helping to reduce fatigue.\n\nFinally, the **stem** (which connects the handlebar to the fork) and overall handlebar height also influence your riding posture and how much weight is on your hands. Adjusting these can contribute to overall comfort.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0025-95-17314:7": 0.6600411534309387, "clueweb22-en0004-98-08500:1": 0.6555467247962952, "clueweb22-en0023-62-08521:2": 0.5731801986694336, "clueweb22-en0035-81-05839:6": 0.4986814558506012, "clueweb22-en0018-20-12295:1": 0.4915989339351654, "clueweb22-en0015-57-16098:6": 0.490387499332428, "clueweb22-en0037-42-19283:4": 0.4902782440185