{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-1_11", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Clipless pedals are definitely worth considering for your mountain bike, especially since you ride on forest trails and rough paths and prioritize comfort. They offer several benefits: your feet stay securely connected to the pedals, which significantly improves pedaling efficiency, especially when climbing or on long rides. This secure connection also provides greater stability and control over rough terrain, reducing the effort needed to keep your feet on the pedals and potentially enhancing comfort by maintaining a consistent foot position. Mountain bike specific clipless pedals, like two-bolt SPD systems, are designed to shed mud and allow for easier walking off the bike, as the cleats are recessed.\n\nHowever, there are a few drawbacks for a casual rider. The main one is the learning curve; it takes practice to clip in and out quickly, which can feel tricky at first, especially on technical downhill sections where you might need to put a foot down fast. You also need to purchase compatible cycling shoes, which is an additional cost. Given you're a casual rider and not very handy with DIY, this initial adjustment might be a consideration.\n\nA good compromise could be hybrid or dual-platform pedals. These offer a flat side for casual rides with regular shoes and a clipless side for when you want the benefits of being clipped in, allowing you to ease into them.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0037-60-07465:4": 0.7695006728172302, "clueweb22-en0001-22-06023:3": 0.7447142004966736, "clueweb22-en0017-66-09315:12": 0.710741400718689, "clueweb22-en0037-60-07465:5": 0.6894006133079529, "clueweb22-en0002-05-13020:4": 0.6553082466125488, "clueweb22-en0000-09-06499:4": 0.6216000914573669, "clueweb22-en0002-05-13020:5": 0.6190911531448364, "clueweb22-en0034-45-11174:7": 0.6014788746833801, "clueweb22-en0036-51-05870:13": 0.