{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-2_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "A bike chain that keeps falling off is a common issue, often related to a few key things.\n\nThe most frequent cause is usually an issue with your bike's derailleurs, specifically the \"limit screws.\" These tiny screws, often labeled \"H\" (High) and \"L\" (Low), control how far your derailleur can move, preventing the chain from shifting past your gears and falling off. If your chain consistently falls off on one side (either towards the wheel or away from the bike), adjusting these screws can often fix it. The \"H\" screw limits the high gears (outer gears), and the \"L\" screw limits the low gears (inner gears). Tightening them can prevent the chain from overshooting.\n\nAnother common reason is a worn-out chain or one with broken or bent links. Over time, chains stretch and wear, which can lead to them not seating properly on the gears and falling off.\n\nGiven that you're not very handy with DIY, these adjustments can be tricky if you're unfamiliar with them. While adjusting limit screws is a basic task for a bike mechanic, it might be best to take your bike to a professional if you're not comfortable doing it yourself. They can quickly diagnose whether it's an adjustment issue or if your chain needs replacement.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0041-27-04552:13": 0.7095151543617249, "clueweb22-en0029-48-06882:15": 0.6434146761894226, "clueweb22-en0020-44-10600:19": 0.5448287129402161, "clueweb22-en0029-48-06882:14": 0.5105827450752258}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0041-27-04552:13": 0.7095151543617249, "clueweb22-en0029-48-06882:15": 0.6434146761894226, "clueweb22-en0042-40-04362:13": 0.6300737857818604, "clueweb22-en0018-28-09667:1": 0.5622552037239075, "clueweb22-en0002-80-07004:6": 0.5547736883163452, "clueweb22-en0020-44-10600:19": 0.5448287129402161, "clueweb22-en0042-04-05719:20": 