{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-2_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "It sounds like your bike chain is coming off frequently, which can often be resolved with a few DIY adjustments.\n\nFirst, if your chain has simply come off, you can re-seat it. Hook the chain onto the rear sprocket, then hook as much as you can onto the bottom of the front sprocket. Carefully turn the pedal backward, and the chain should catch and begin to turn around the front sprocket. Elevating the bike's rear wheel can make this easier.\n\nNext, check if your chain is worn out. Measure 12 links; the distance between those pins should be 12 inches (30.48 centimeters). If the distance is more than 12 1/8 inches (30.80 centimeters), your chain is stretched and needs replacement. A worn chain is a common reason for it to skip or fall off.\n\nIf the chain consistently falls off in the same direction, you likely need to adjust your derailleur's limit screws. These tiny screws, labeled \"H\" (High) and \"L\" (Low) on both front and back derailleurs, tell the derailleur how far to move. Turn the \"H\" screws clockwise to prevent the chain from moving too far right, and \"L\" screws clockwise to prevent it from moving too far left. Adjust them so the derailleur lines up in the middle of the gear when in the furthest gear.\n\nFinally, if your chain is skipping before it falls off, the issue might be incorrect cable tension for your derailleur. The most typical recommendation for fixing a skipping chain is to add tension to the rear derailleur.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0020-44-10600:19": 0.9098073244094849, "clueweb22-en0019-70-04653:6": 0.8907049298286438, "clueweb22-en0022-62-09551:1": 0.8884627819061279, "clueweb22-en0010-76-17069:2": 0.8728018999099731}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0020-44-10600:11": 0.9380493760108948, "clueweb22-en0025-47-05040:1": 0.9313637614250183, "clueweb22-en0018-