{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-2_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "It's generally not recommended to use car oil to lubricate your bike chain. Motor oils are typically too thick and do not penetrate the chain thoroughly, which can cause excessive resistance, reduce chain life, and impair shifting performance (Passages 8, 9, 11). They are also not designed to repel dirt and water as effectively as bicycle-specific lubricants (Passage 9).\n\nFor your bike chain, it's best to use a lubricant specifically designed for bicycles. These are formulated to minimize dirt accumulation, provide durability, and ensure smooth operation (Passage 9, 11).\n\nGiven that you bike in rainy weather and forests, an \"all-conditions\" or \"wet\" chain lube would be a good choice. Some options include:\n*   **Wet lubes** like WD-40 Specialist Bike Wet Chain Lube, which forms a durable coating to repel mud and water (Passage 18).\n*   **All-conditions lubes** like ProGoldProlink Chain Lube or Park Tool CL-1 Synthetic Blend (PTFE) (Passage 7, 16).\n*   For a more environmentally friendly option, Muc Off Dry Lube is a wax-based formula that is fully biodegradable (Passage 6).\n\nUsing a dedicated bike chain lubricant will ensure optimal performance and extend the life of your chain and drivetrain components.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0019-98-16645:14": 0.8271898031234741, "clueweb22-en0024-04-10077:3": 0.8186207413673401, "clueweb22-en0024-04-10077:2": 0.8049017786979675, "clueweb22-en0027-12-01183:2": 0.8853946924209595, "clueweb22-en0043-15-14227:3": 0.8364306092262268, "clueweb22-en0011-31-03597:5": 0.8305841088294983, "clueweb22-en0011-31-03597:7": 0.8274859189987183, "clueweb22-en0019-98-16645:13": 0.7985544204711914, "clueweb22-en0011-31-03597:1": 0.7836694717407227, "clueweb22-en0011-31-03597:4": 0.7826257944107056}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0030-04-19030:20": 0.8965176